E=[
(1,1885,"Sky","A giant slow object near Adrianople","An object four or five times the size of the Moon is seen moving slowly across the sky near Adrianople. L'Astronomie reported it, and Fort lists it among large slow objects seen at night.","BD","Book of the Damned, citing L'Astronomie, 1886-309","object four or five times size of moon, moving slowly across sky, Nov. 1, 1885, near Adrianople"),
(2,1819,"Falls","Red rain at Blankenberge","A red rain falls at Blankenberge, Holland, a week before the black rain and earthquake of Canada. Two chemists of Bruges concentrated 144 ounces of it to 4 and found no sand. Fort cites the Annals of Philosophy.","BD","Book of the Damned, citing Annals of Philosophy, 16-226","there fell, at Blankenberge, Holland, a red rain"),
(3,1886,"Sky","A bright white cloud over Hamar","At night a bright white cloud over Hamar, Norway emits brilliant rays of light, drifts across the sky and 'retained throughout its original form'. Fort cites Nature.","BD","Book of the Damned, citing Nature, Dec. 16, 1886, p. 158","Nov. 3, 1886, at Hamar, Norway"),
(4,1883,"Cosmos","Misty light on the dark Moon","Prof. John Haywood sees misty light effects on the dark part of the Moon, not like earthshine. Fort points to the year 1883, when extraordinary atmospheric effects began, and suspects the Moon is affected by Earth's atmosphere.","NL","New Lands, citing Sidereal Messenger, 3-121","similar appearances upon the moon, Nov. 4, 1883"),
(5,1883,"Sky","A moon-sized light over Chile","A luminous object the size of the full Moon is visible for an hour and a half over Chile. Fort cites Comptes Rendus in a list of such large slow lights.","BD","Book of the Damned, citing Comptes Rendus, 103-682","luminous object, size of full moon, visible an hour and a half, Chili, Nov. 5, 1883"),
(6,1866,"Sky","A ship's red light in the sky of Cartagena","The British Consul's office at Cartagena, Colombia reports a luminous object moving in the sky 'of the magnitude, color, and brilliance of a ship's red light'. The Times printed Senor De Fonblanque's account.","NL","New Lands, citing London Times, Jan. 2, 1867","an account, in the London Times, Jan. 2, 1867, by Senor De Fonblanque"),
(7,1891,"Cosmos","A luminous point in Aristarchus","M. d'Adjuda of the Lisbon Observatory sees 'a very distinct, luminous point' in the lunar crater Aristarchus. Fort calls it a long time since the previous such light, in 1867.","NL","New Lands, citing L'Astronomie, 11-33","The light was seen, Nov. 7, 1891, by M. d'Adjuda"),
(9,1819,"Falls","The black rain of Canada","A black rain falls in Canada, accompanied by 'shocks like those' of an earthquake. Orthodoxy blamed forest fires south of the Ohio River. Fort cites Zurcher's Meteors.","BD","Book of the Damned, citing Zurcher, Meteors, p. 238","the black rain of Canada, Nov. 9, 1819"),
(10,1883,"Sky","A comet with two tails","A correspondent to the American Meteorological Journal reports an object like a comet, with two tails, one up and one down. Fort files it with torpedo-shaped bodies seen in the sky.","BD","Book of the Damned, citing Amer. Met. Jour., 1-110","Nov. 10 or 12, 1883"),
(11,1911,"Falls","Hailstones that exploded like pistol shots","Large hailstones at the University of Missouri explode with sounds like pistol shots. The writer had seen the same eighteen years before at Lexington, Kentucky. Nature published his note.","BD","Book of the Damned, citing Nature, 88-350","Large hailstones noted at the University of Missouri, Nov. 11, 1911: they exploded with sounds like pistol shots"),
(12,1902,"Falls","The greatest fall of matter in Australia","On Nov. 12 and 13 occurs the greatest fall of matter in Australian history, and on the 14th it 'rained mud' in Tasmania. Fort notes it was blamed on whirlwinds.","BD","Book of the Damned, on the Australian falls of 1902","Upon Nov. 12 and 13, 1902, occurred the greatest fall of matter in the history of Australia"),
(13,1833,"Sky","The Leonid storm","Over the night of Nov. 13-14, fiery meteors gush from the heavens for six hours along the whole Atlantic coast of the United States. Fort calls it the most sensational celestial spectacle of the nineteenth century.","NL","New Lands, on the meteor storm of 1833","Upon the night of November 13-14, 1833, occurred the most sensational celestial spectacle of the nineteenth century"),
(14,1901,"Falls","Pound-weight ice in a Victoria tornado","Lumps of ice one pound in weight fall in a tornado in Victoria, Australia. Fort cites the Meteorology of Australia and says tornadoes might bring such ice down.","BD","Book of the Damned, citing Meteorology of Australia, p. 34","lumps of ice one pound in weight, Nov. 14, 1901, in a tornado, Victoria"),
(15,1895,"Mysteries","An explosion near Fenchurch Street","At noon an 'alarming explosion' is heard near Fenchurch Street, London. No damage is done and no trace of anything that exploded is found. An hour later another occurs near the Mansion House.","NL","New Lands, citing London Morning Post, Nov. 16, 1895","occurred somewhere near Fenchurch Street, London"),
(16,1857,"Falls","Black capillary matter at Charleston","'Black, capillary matter' falls at Charleston, South Carolina. Fort cites the American Journal of Science in a list of miscellaneous strange substances.","BD","Book of the Damned, citing Amer. Jour. Sci., 2-31-459","that fell, Nov. 16, 1857, at Charleston, S.C."),
(17,1882,"Sky","Maunder's strange celestial visitor","At Greenwich during an aurora, E. W. Maunder sees a great circular disk of greenish light move smoothly across the sky. Fort believes a vast dirigible crossed England that night, with many observations to match.","BD","Book of the Damned, citing Observatory, 39-214","Maunder was at the Royal Observatory, Greenwich, Nov. 17, 1882, at night"),
(18,1850,"Falls","Worms on the snow at Sangerfield","A large number of worms are found in a snowstorm on the surface of snow about four inches thick near Sangerfield, New York. The writer thought rain had brought them to the surface.","BD","Book of the Damned, citing Scientific American, 6-96","near Sangerfield, N.Y., Nov. 18, 1850"),
(19,1899,"Falls","A meteorite in a storm off Greece","A meteorite falls in a thunderstorm off the coast of Greece. Fort cites Nature, in a list of meteorites that fell with storms.","BD","Book of the Damned, citing Nature, 61-111","meteorite in a thunderstorm, off coast of Greece, Nov. 19, 1899"),
(21,1898,"Falls","Spider-web strands fall on Montgomery","Numerous batches of a spider-web-like substance fall at Montgomery, Alabama, in strands and masses several inches long and broad. The writer said it was not spiders' web. The Monthly Weather Review quoted the Advertiser.","BD","Book of the Damned, citing Monthly Weather Review, 26-566","upon Nov. 21, 1898, numerous batches of spider-web-like substance fell in Montgomery"),
(22,1821,"Mysteries","A luminous meteor with the Naples shock","A luminous meteor moves in the same direction as the shock at Naples. Fort lists it among many lights in the sky that accompany earthquakes in Mallet's catalogue.","BD","Book of the Damned, citing Mallet's catalogue","Naples, Nov. 22, 1821"),
(23,1886,"Mysteries","One son vanishes at Edina","At Edina, Missouri, a man and his three sons are pulling corn when something said to be lightning flashes from the sky. The man is injured, one son killed, one seriously hurt, and the third disappears. The Charleston News and Courier reported it.","NL","New Lands, citing Charleston News and Courier, Nov. 25, 1886","at Edina, Mo., Nov. 23, a man and his three sons were pulling corn on a farm"),
(25,1894,"Cosmos","A light near Mars","At the Lowell Observatory Prof. Pickering and others see a light-reflecting body, or bright spot, near Mars above an unilluminated part of the planet. It looks self-luminous.","BD","Book of the Damned, citing Astrophysical Journal, 1-127","seen Nov. 25, 1894, by Prof. Pickering and others, at the Lowell Observatory"),
(27,1919,"Mysteries","A shock and a blinding glare in Michigan","A shock rocks Michigan and many people rush from their homes thinking it an earthquake. In Indiana, Illinois and Michigan a 'blinding glare' is seen in the sky. The New York Times reported it.","NL","New Lands, citing New York Times, Nov. 28, 1919","There was a similar shock in Michigan, Nov. 27, 1919"),
(28,1872,"Cosmos","Klinkerfues telegraphs Madras","Prof. Klinkerfues of Gottingen, looking for Biela's comet, sees meteors in its expected path and telegraphs Pogson at Madras to look near Theta Centauri. Pogson announced he saw what he was told to see.","NL","New Lands, on Klinkerfues and Pogson","upon Nov. 28, 1872, Prof. Klinkerfues, of Göttingen, looking for Biela's comet"),
(29,1831,"Sky","A moon-sized fireball over Thuringia","A fireball of about the apparent size of the Moon appears over Thuringerwald. Fort lists it among strange sky events preceding earthquakes in Mallet's catalogue.","BD","Book of the Damned, citing Mallet's catalogue","Thuringerwald, Nov. 29, 1831"),
(30,1880,"Sky","Winged bodies cross the Sun at Palermo","Signor Ricco of the Palermo Observatory, watching the Sun at 8:30 a.m., sees bodies in two long parallel lines and a shorter one slowly traverse its disk. The bodies looked winged to him.","BD","Book of the Damned, citing L'Astronomie, 1887-66","Nov. 30, 1880, at 8:30 o'clock in the morning, he was watching the sun"),
]
